        {
          "subsection_id": "abg_analyser_subsection_13",
          "subsection_title": "12. Common OSCE questions and model answers",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "abg_analyser_group_19",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q1. What is an ABG analyzer?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It is a diagnostic instrument that measures blood gas parameters and acid–base status from a blood specimen."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q2. What are the three principal ABG measurements?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "pH, PaCO₂ and PaO₂. Bicarbonate and other parameters are also commonly reported."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q3. What is the difference between the ABG syringe and analyzer?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The syringe collects and preserves the sample; the analyzer measures its parameters and produces the result."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q4. Why is quality control important?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "It helps detect analytical problems and provides confidence that results are sufficiently reliable for clinical use."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q5. What causes an analyzer to reject a sample?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Depending on the device, causes may include insufficient sample volume, clotting, cartridge problems, sampling errors or other instrument-detected faults."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q6. Why must oxygen therapy be documented?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "PaO₂ depends partly on inspired oxygen concentration. Without knowing the patient's oxygen support, interpreting oxygenation can be misleading."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q7. Does a normal pH exclude an acid–base disorder?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "No. Mixed disorders or compensated disturbances may produce a pH within the reference interval. PaCO₂, bicarbonate and the clinical context must also be assessed."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q8. What should you do with a critical result?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Verify patient and sample identity, assess whether the result is credible, promptly communicate it according to local policy and support urgent clinical management."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "abg_analyser_subsection_14",
          "subsection_title": "13. Common mistakes and examiner tips",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "abg_analyser_group_20",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Common mistakes"
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Confusing the analyzer with the sampling syringe.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Assuming every model measures the same parameters.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Ignoring calibration and quality-control status.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Analyzing clotted or unsuitable specimens.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Failing to record oxygen or ventilator settings.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Treating a calculated bicarbonate value as necessarily directly measured.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Interpreting the result without considering the patient's condition.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Failing to communicate critical findings.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Examiner tips"
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Identify the instrument and explain its role in critical care.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Name pH, PaCO₂ and PaO₂ as core parameters.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Mention additional parameters only when supported by the analyzer.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Explain why sample handling and quality control matter.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "State that results must be interpreted alongside oxygen support and clinical findings.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Distinguish measured values from calculated parameters when asked.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
